stage: 4
finding: P1 — The calibrated statistics leave an unqualified localized-error blind spot in the step oracle. The full humanoid's thresholds are count <=52 and largest ratio <=62.3, and tests/step-oracle.gpu.test.ts:465-472 accepts exactly that rectangle. With the reported actual port count 0 and ratio 0.984, a localized continuous error producing one out-of-bound quantity at, for example, ten times its own tolerance still passes BOTH statistics. Up to 52 such quantities can pass. The count does not compensate for the global ratio threshold's one rare null outlier: that outlier grants its 62.3 allowance to every quantity at every step, including quantities with tight null distributions. This is the same class of sparse error that motivated adding the ratio statistic; the million-times-floor fault probe only tests the far end and does not establish sensitivity inside this accepted region. The maximum is a mathematically valid conservative order statistic; deleting/clipping its outlier after observation would not be a valid fix. Before treating this as stage 4's per-step bug oracle, independently characterize this null outlier (location, bound/model, discrete/flip path) and demonstrate localized-fault detection at affected and unaffected quantities, or predeclare more localized/power-sensitive statistics and recalibrate. If accepting this sensitivity loss is intentional, the weakened oracle contract needs explicit approval rather than inheriting the earlier claim that a port bug appears where it enters. CESM's ensemble-consistency precedent supplies a null-calibration approach, not a power guarantee for these two particular statistics. Statistics assessment: for iid null scores with the same scoring map, the 598 maximum is a valid upper tolerance limit: P(tail >0.005) <=0.995^598 <0.05 (inequality, not necessarily equality with ties). No independence between count and ratio is needed to union-bound their tail probabilities. The conditional high-confidence statements are individually 95%; both holding is guaranteed only at >=90% by another union bound, not 95%. If an overall 95%-confidence/1%-tail claim is intended, allocate the confidence budget too (e.g. 736 samples for alpha=0.025 per statistic). Alternatively the exchangeable fresh-score UNCONDITIONAL rank bound is <=2/599 for the two statistics; state explicitly which guarantee is claimed. Neither bounds false acceptance of port bugs. Per-quantity qualification and the ensemble null test address the earlier incorrect inheritance of per-quantity guarantees by a schedule, but do not remove the power finding above. Resolved: full humanoid qualification is now a real test, not a passing todo. The reported 9-pass/exit-0 qualification has normal regression semantics. Keyed bound/null seeds, fresh child execs, and exact-state/build/code cache keys remove the cache-history dependence identified earlier. Narrow independent run: 5 oracle tests pass, 943 assertions, 5.17s (rounded-normal test; IEEE/native; request-alone repeatability after unrelated requests; distinct/repeatable null runs; cold/warm/partial spread caches). Inspected dbe849d: --log-driver=none only suppresses daemon retention of stdout, leaving attached protocol output intact; no identified arithmetic or seeding effect. Ragdoll short hold independently rerun: PASS, count 0/0; ratio 0.9599998593338618 <=0.9600000977526473, at step 1/world 0/contact 5 anchor A, derived RANGE tolerance 0.125 (not the floor). Null median 0.30477576898301584, p99 0.9600000083453002, max 0.9600000977526473. This is a nearly tied discrete-valued statistic, not evidence for fitting a threshold or reason to add epsilon: keyed seeding makes the strict comparison reproducible. It is a useful margin warning, not a separate finding. No full 3h52m qualification, pre-land check, or hosted-CI run; no code edits. Public precedent checked: Baker et al. 2015 GMD 8:2829 and Milroy et al. 2018 GMD 11:697.
